1. Introduction
DAT250 SecureApp is an intentionally vulnerable FastAPI-based web application that simulates a university student project portal with course registration, assignment submission, grading, profile management, and internal messaging. The application was run locally through Docker and assessed only within the authorised laboratory environment.
The purpose of this assignment was to map the application, identify security weaknesses using manual and automated techniques, reproduce selected vulnerabilities, and explain their potential effect on a real student information system. The supplied evidence supports seven distinct evidence-backed security findings across access control, authentication, injection, file/resource handling, and business logic. Additional configuration and browser-hardening weaknesses were recorded by OWASP ZAP.
This report separates demonstrated behaviour from plausible but unproven impact. The supplied material does not prove an unauthorised password-reset workflow or server-side execution of an uploaded PHP file, so those outcomes are not reported as confirmed exploits. The upload finding is limited to the behaviour actually shown: an unexpected PHP file was accepted and listed by the application.
2. Testing Methodology
2.1 Scope and ethical boundaries
Target: the local DAT250 SecureApp instance at http://localhost:18181 (equivalently http://127.0.0.1:18181). Testing was restricted to the provided Docker lab, provided test accounts, and disposable test data. Real UiS systems, public systems, external hosts, third-party services, and other students' machines were out of scope.
The assignment provides administrator, teacher, teaching-assistant, and student roles. The supplied evidence directly shows student/standard-user and teaching-assistant testing, while teacher and administrator records appear as target data and privileged functionality. Because a complete authenticated session for every role is not visible in the supplied evidence, this report does not claim that every role was manually exercised.
2.2 Assessment approach
Endpoint and role mapping: browse the application under different roles and record routes, forms, identifiers, and privilege boundaries.
Manual request manipulation: intercept and replay HTTP requests to determine whether server-side authorization matches the restrictions shown in the user interface.
Authentication testing: issue repeated login requests and observe status codes, response behaviour, and whether throttling or temporary lockout occurs.
Input testing: manipulate message content and the course-search parameter to test for cross-site scripting and SQL injection.
File and business-logic testing: upload unexpected file types and replay a previously valid assignment-upload request to observe duplicate state changes.
Automated scanning: run OWASP ZAP against the local target and use its passive findings as supporting evidence rather than treating scanner alerts as automatically confirmed exploits.
